ce': [], 'tests': tests}\\n    # ddmin\\n    def ddmin(changes):\\n        # recursive helper with granularity\\n        def _ddmin(s, n):\\n            if len(s) == 0:\\n                return []\\n            if len(s) == 1:\\n                if test(s):\\n                    return s[:]\\n                else:\\n                    return []\\n            # split into n subsets\\n            subset_size = (len(s) + n - 1) // n\\n            subsets = [s[i:i+subset_size] for i in range(0, len(s), subset_size)]\\n            # test each subset\\n            for sub in subsets:\\n                if test(sub):\\n                    return _ddmin(sub, 2)\\n            # test each complement\\n            for sub in subsets:\\n                complement = [x for x in s if x not in sub]  # preserve order\\n                if test(complement):\\n                    return _ddmin(complement, 2)\\n            # increase granularity if possible\\n            if n < len(s):\\n                return _ddmin(s, min(2*n, len(s)))\\n            else:\\n                return s[:]\\n        return _ddmin(changes, 2)\\n    isolated = ddmin(diff)\\n    return {'isolated_difference': isolated, 'tests': tests}\"\n}\n\nMake sure no trailing commas, correct escaping. Also note that we used single quotes inside the Python code; that's fine. We need to escape the double quotes in the JSON string only if they appear inside the value. Our Python code uses only single quotes, so no need to escape double quotes. But we used double quotes for the outer JSON keys. That's fine. Also backslashes in Python code: we have newlines as \\n in the string representation. Also we have backslash in \"// n\" but that's fine. Also we have \\n in \"def solve...\". Actually when we write the string in JSON, we need to escape backslashes and quotes. In this code, there are no backslashes except for the newline escapes we manually insert. So we write the Python code with actual newlines inside the JSON string? 